WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.359
Everyone thinks anarchy is.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.609
Do whatever you want.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.739
A massive lesson

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.359
that I have gotten out of telling

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.049
this story.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.119
Anarchy just means self-ownership.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.949
The most peaceful ideology in

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:14.609
the world.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.439
Was that there really

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.869
is no panacea.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.429
Fuck the government.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.019
Most of our people were buying into

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.020
bitcoin.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.190
Coming in on private jets.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.539
That is going to solve everything.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.369
This beautiful paradise can

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:32.169
be a beacon of freedom for people to

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.170
migrate to.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.579
What happens when a bunch of radical

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.139
individualists try to start a

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.279
community.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.280
In Acapulco

00:00:41.619 --> 00:00:42.249
Biggest Anarchist conference.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.779
Trip to Mexico.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:44.949
I'm really excited.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.659
That is the subject of a new six

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.489
part HBO docu series

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.379
from director Todd Schramke and

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.149
his wife and co-producer Kim

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.069
Kylland. The pair spent six

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.529
years documenting and Acapulco

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.599
an annual conference for anarchists

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.029
started in Mexico by Jeff Berwick,

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.589
a political commentator who calls

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.568
himself the Dollar Vigilante.

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.639
All fiat currencies going to zero.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.139
All markets just collapse and

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.039
closing.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.899
I talked with Schramke about

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.669
how making the film has shaped his

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.679
view of anarchism and

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.539
libertarianism and what

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.429
the story of Anarchapulco can

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.529
teach us about how to launch

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.269
successful experiments in

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.129
alternative living.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:25.989
I also asked him to reflect back on

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.459
when he found out one of its

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.379
attendees, a man who called himself

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.353
John Galton was murdered.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:33.939
The anarchist, has been shot to

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.199
death. Tragic situation.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.939
Rumors spread that other members of

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:38.529
the anarchist community may have

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:39.729
been involved.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.499
There was some sketchy stuff

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.699
happening behind the scenes.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.809
It really was one of the most

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.999
emotionally complicated

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.009
few weeks of my life.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.979
I, I

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.019
mean, my wife and I

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.089
were that morning, the morning

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.979
of the murder going back and

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.689
forth with John and Lily and the

00:01:58.690 --> 00:01:59.949
Freemans. We were texting all

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.719
morning. We were just two weeks

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:02.949
away, less than two weeks.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.449
We're about a week away from our

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:05.439
next production trip.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.299
And yeah, it was

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.309
all of a sudden they all stopped

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.689
responding.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.789
And it was just shortly after

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.859
that I turned on Facebook

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.169
and saw that horrific footage

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.209
of Lily on her livestream.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.399
It was just this really,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.409
really intense feeling of knowing

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.269
this was going to change my life

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:26.979
in so many ways.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.959
And the reason it was so emotionally

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.879
complicated is because

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.139
and it's one of the worst things

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.869
about being a documentary filmmaker

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.389
is that often other people's tragedy

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.280
can work to your advantage,

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.429
which is really blunt

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:43.929
way to put it. But it's true.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.299
I mean, I knew this was going to

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.039
bring this story to a whole new

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.229
level of complexity and

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.299
interest, and it proved to be true.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.249
And we were the

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:55.929
only people out there.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.909
We had a small social media presence

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.399
at the time that was mostly for

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.809
engaging with the community and

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.069
people going to the conference so

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:04.599
they could know who we were.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.579
And but because of that,

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.979
we were the only real outside

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.809
documentation of the fact that there

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.759
was this local community

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.649
that had emerged in Acapulco.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.240
So we very quickly started

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.479
dealing with a lot of

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.399
press, reaching out and

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.409
trying to get our take on the story,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.659
all while trying to maintain

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.429
and follow what was going on within

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.349
the community and

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:31.209
dealing with our friends and family,

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.489
all begging us not to go to Acapulco

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:35.319
again and fearing for

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.569
our own lives and safety.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:38.409
It's been difficult.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:39.309
It's been challenging.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.279
It's it's fun at times, but

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.359
it has it's also made me question

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.429
if documentary

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:46.899
film is what I really want to be

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:48.879
doing, because it's

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.829
one ethical dilemma after

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.719
another. And in

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.669
some ways, that's what makes it more

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.459
powerful than a scripted

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.289
show. But it also comes

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:01.059
with a lot of struggle.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.229
You said a lot of media

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.209
reached out to you after John's

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.499
death. You portray the framing

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.679
that the media was running

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.539
with. It's kind of obvious, you

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.849
know, an anarchist gets murdered

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.879
in Mexico doing anarchy

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.929
was. And that was kind of like

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.018
the the headline

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:22.889
that that you would see some version

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:23.939
of that.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.409
Some people featured in the

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.959
documentary like Jeff Berwick

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.939
would say, you know

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.559
this his death really didn't have

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.850
anything to do with Anarchapulco

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.559
or anarchy.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.559
It was kind of like his.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.659
What he was doing outside of that

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.489
is what brought

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.079
that in.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.149
There's this whole mystery

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.039
involving a guy

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.079
who may have been involved with

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.039
the hit that was put on him.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.229
Like, how do you think about that?

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.329
How do you think about what

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.249
happened to John

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.169
and the relationship

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.299
with an Anarchapulco and,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.459
you know, anarchism more

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.460
broadly?

